## The Solution

### Built together, one decision at a time.

We started with a moodboard not just a mood wall, but a real creative direction in visual form. Soft pastels, spa-like references, minimal layouts. The founder was in the room with us from the beginning: reacting, redirecting, making real decisions at every stage.

Then the print proofs arrived. The soft palette beautiful on screen fell completely flat on paper. So we shifted. Warmer, earthier tones. Amber gold for the hair care line. Terracotta and dusty rose for skincare. The colors got richer and suddenly the packaging felt as warm and credible as the ingredients inside it.

Illustrations weren't in the original brief. But as the layouts took shape, we both felt the packaging needed a human presence not a pattern, not a diagram, just a woman in her ritual. We pitched it. The client said yes. We sketched rough concepts, explored variations with AI tools, and drew the final characters by hand in Illustrator. Each figure specific to her product her gesture, her world.

From there, we built 3D product visuals in Blender for listings and presentations, and created the complete Amazon A+ content suite. Everything the brand needed to show up properly in hand and on screen.

### 01. Pastels looked great on screen. Print told a different story.

Multiple physical proofs pulled by us and the client together until the color felt as rich in hand as it did on a monitor.

### 02. Two lines, two palettes, one brand that holds together.

Amber gold for hair care. Terracotta rose for skincare. Different enough to tell apart, similar enough to belong together.

### 03. Illustrations were never in the brief until we made the case.

Rough sketches, AI explorations, final artwork drawn by hand in Illustrator. Each character drawn for her specific product.

### 04. The wordmark is the design. Everything else is supporting cast.

A tall vertical "THE IMMUNO CO." anchors every face of every pack. Architecture before decoration.

